It is a sequence of stages where each stage transforms the stream of documents: $match filters, $group aggregates, $project reshapes, $sort/$limit order and trim, $unwind flattens arrays and $lookup joins another collection. Example – top customers by revenue:
db.orders.aggregate([
  { $match: { status: "PAID" } },
  { $group: { _id: "$customerId", revenue: { $sum: "$total" } } },
  { $sort: { revenue: -1 } }, { $limit: 5 }
]);

| updateOne ($set, $inc) | replaceOne | findOneAndUpdate | repository.save()
What is sent | Only the operators | A whole new document | Only the operators | The whole entity
Returns | Matched / modified counts | Matched / modified counts | The document, before the change by default | The saved entity
Lost-update risk | Low: changes only named fields | High if you read, edit and replace | Low | High, unless the entity has @Version
Spring API | updateFirst | findAndReplace | findAndModify | MongoRepository.save
Typical use | Increment a counter, push to an array | Overwrite a settings document | Claim the next job, reserve stock and read the result | Simple CRUD screens
Query q = Query.query(Criteria.where("_id").is(id).and("stock").gt(0));
Update u = new Update().inc("stock", -1);
Product after = mongoTemplate.findAndModify(q, u,
    FindAndModifyOptions.options().returnNew(true), Product.class);
// null means: out of stock (no document matched)
Rule of thumb: every one of these is atomic on a single document; the difference is how much you overwrite. Prefer operator updates for concurrent changes, and add @Version wherever you keep using save().
